1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,697
Every no-show is an empty
chair you can't get back.

2
00:00:03,697 --> 00:00:08,650
In this lesson, you'll see the three
appointment workflows that most businesses

3
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:11,910
need: confirmations and
reminders, no-show recovery,

4
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:14,230
and a review request after the visit.

5
00:00:15,091 --> 00:00:18,118
Bloom keeps these in a
folder called Appointments.

6
00:00:18,118 --> 00:00:22,415
Grouping workflows by purpose like
this makes them much easier to find,

7
00:00:22,415 --> 00:00:25,442
especially once you have
twenty or thirty of them.

8
00:00:26,291 --> 00:00:29,512
Let's open the confirmation
and reminders workflow.

9
00:00:29,512 --> 00:00:34,438
It's triggered by Customer Booked
Appointment, filtered to specific calendars.

10
00:00:34,438 --> 00:00:39,679
That way, a new patient consultation and
a check-up can each have their own wording

11
00:00:39,679 --> 00:00:40,437
if you want.

12
00:00:41,512 --> 00:00:44,770
The first action confirms
the booking straight away.

13
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:47,025
Notice the appointment custom value.

14
00:00:47,025 --> 00:00:51,286
Kyntlo fills in the real date and
time of each person's appointment,

15
00:00:51,286 --> 00:00:54,920
so one message works for every
booking, whatever the date.

16
00:00:55,771 --> 00:00:57,080
Now the clever part.

17
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:59,045
This wait isn't a fixed delay.

18
00:00:59,045 --> 00:01:02,646
It's set to Event, or
Appointment Time: one day before.

19
00:01:02,646 --> 00:01:06,050
Whether someone books three
weeks ahead or tomorrow,

20
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:09,520
the reminder always lands
the day before their visit.

21
00:01:11,094 --> 00:01:15,829
After the day-before reminder, a second
wait sends one more text two hours before

22
00:01:15,829 --> 00:01:17,817
the appointment, with the address.

23
00:01:17,817 --> 00:01:21,792
Two reminders like this can make a
big difference to your show rate,

24
00:01:21,792 --> 00:01:24,423
and your team never has
to send them by hand.

25
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:27,284
One setting matters a lot here.

26
00:01:27,284 --> 00:01:29,946
Open Settings and turn on Allow re-entry.

27
00:01:29,946 --> 00:01:33,321
Patients book again and
again, and without re-entry,

28
00:01:33,321 --> 00:01:37,476
they'd only ever get reminders
for their very first appointment.

29
00:01:38,327 --> 00:01:39,278
Next, no-shows.

30
00:01:39,278 --> 00:01:43,779
This workflow uses the Appointment
Status trigger, filtered to No Show.

31
00:01:43,779 --> 00:01:47,900
When your team marks an appointment
as a no-show in the calendar,

32
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,880
the contact enters this
workflow automatically.

33
00:01:51,731 --> 00:01:54,401
It tags the contact, waits thirty minutes,

34
00:01:54,401 --> 00:01:57,515
then sends a friendly text
with a link to rebook.

35
00:01:57,515 --> 00:01:59,549
No guilt, just an easy way back.

36
00:01:59,549 --> 00:02:04,824
A day later, if they still haven't rebooked,
it creates a task for someone to call.

37
00:02:05,674 --> 00:02:08,864
The third workflow runs
when the status is Showed.

38
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:14,224
Two hours after the visit, while the
experience is fresh, it sends a review request.

39
00:02:14,224 --> 00:02:17,670
We'll go deeper into reviews
in the reputation module.

40
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:19,475
One last check.

41
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:24,568
In Global Workflow Settings, Stop on
response removes people from workflows when

42
00:02:24,568 --> 00:02:25,268
they reply.

43
00:02:25,268 --> 00:02:30,552
That's great for sales follow-ups, but a
patient who texts back thanks should still

44
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:31,825
get their reminders.

45
00:02:31,825 --> 00:02:34,307
So exclude your reminder workflow here.

46
00:02:35,158 --> 00:02:36,249
Here's a pro tip.

47
00:02:36,249 --> 00:02:41,254
The no-show and review workflows only
run when someone updates the appointment

48
00:02:41,254 --> 00:02:41,703
status.

49
00:02:41,703 --> 00:02:46,965
Make it part of your team's end-of-day
routine to mark every appointment as showed

50
00:02:46,965 --> 00:02:47,671
or no-show.

51
00:02:47,671 --> 00:02:50,623
Thirty seconds a day
keeps everything running.

52
00:02:51,479 --> 00:02:53,613
Your calendar now looks after itself.

53
00:02:53,613 --> 00:02:57,132
In the next lesson, we'll connect
workflows to your pipeline,

54
00:02:57,132 --> 00:03:01,401
so deals move forward automatically,
and nothing slips through the cracks.
